## 4. Evidence & Insights

### Confirmed Facts

- **CEFR descriptors are language-independent by design.** The Council of Europe states CEFR is “potentially applicable to all the languages taught in Europe and does not relate to any specific one.” This validates the abstract node model.
- **Referencial Camões PLE exists** as the authoritative Portuguese RLD (Reference Level Description), providing A1–C1 vocabulary and grammar inventories already mapped to CEFR.
- **Open-source multi-language CEFR content exists** (`astrapi69/adaptive-learner-content`), proving the source→target language-pair data model works in practice.
- **Astro is the recommended framework** for content-heavy sites with targeted interactivity, offering zero JavaScript by default and islands for interactive components .

### Assumptions (To Validate)

| Assumption | Validation Plan |
|---|---|
| Learners will tolerate a self-paced platform without human feedback. | Interview 5–8 learners who have used self-paced tools; ask about abandonment reasons. |
| The SRS dashboard will be sufficient motivation for the credential-seeker. | Prototype the dashboard and test with 3 target users. |
| Extracting Referencial Camões data into YAML is feasible within 2–4 weeks. | Spike: extract 5 A1 nodes manually, estimate time per node, extrapolate. |
| Learners will contribute content (lessons) if the schema is simple. | Draft `CONTRIBUTING.md` and test with 1–2 non-technical native speakers. |

### Judgments

- The **abstract content model is the highest-value architectural investment** and the highest-risk component. If the Zod schemas for CEFR nodes and realisations are correct, everything downstream follows. If wrong, every subsequent issue inherits the problem.
- **A1 EN→PT is the correct MVP scope.** Multi-language support should not be built until the A1 content model is proven with real learners.

